      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4.\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3.\n\nWe can express these conditions mathematically as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nFirst, let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we have:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\\), we can write:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), which gives:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to determine how many coins are left when \\( N = 28 \\) are equally divided among 7 people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\]\nSo, the number of coins left when equally divided among 7 people is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
