      "text": "Given:\n1. When the coins are divided among six people, four coins are left over.\n2. When the coins are divided among five people, three coins are left over.\n\nLet \\( N \\) represent the number of coins in the box.\n\nFrom the first condition, we have:\n\\[\nN \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second condition, we have:\n\\[\nN \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nWe are tasked with finding the smallest \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. We will use the method of successive substitutions to find \\( N \\).\n\nFirst, express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( N = 6k + 4 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\text{(since } -1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5})\n\\]\n\nReduce \\( 6 \\mod 5 \\):\n\\[\n6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nThis means \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into \\( N = 6k + 4 \\):\n\\[\nN = 6(5m + 4) + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nN = 30m + 24 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( N \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28\n\\]\n\nWe need the smallest positive \\( N \\). Set \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[\nN = 30 \\cdot 0 + 28 = 28\n\\]\n\nSo, the smallest number of coins is \\( N = 28 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 28 is divided by 7:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of } 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of coins left when equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
