      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3. This can be written as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe start by expressing \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k.\n\\]\nWe substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we obtain:\n\\[\nk \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}.\n\\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[\nN = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28.\n\\]\nThus, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28.\n\\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), so:\n\\[\nN = 28.\n\\]\nWe need to check that this value of \\( N \\) meets both original conditions:\n1. When divided by 6, \\( 28 \\div 6 = 4 \\) remainder \\( 4 \\), so \\( 28 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\).\n2. When divided by 5, \\( 28 \\div 5 = 5 \\) remainder \\( 3 \\), so \\( 28 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\).\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so the smallest number of coins is 28.\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 28 is divided by 7:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 0.\n\\]\nThus, the number of coins left when they are equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]",
